171:    /// The file name. This is the default sorting.
172:    Name(SortCase),
173:
174:    /// The file’s extension, with extensionless files being listed first.
175:    Extension(SortCase),
176:
177:    /// The file’s size, in bytes.
178:    Size,
179:
180:    /// The file’s inode, which usually corresponds to the order in which
181:    /// files were created on the filesystem, more or less.
182:    #[cfg(unix)]
183:    FileInode,
184:
185:    /// The time the file was modified (the “mtime”).
186:    ///
187:    /// As this is stored as a Unix timestamp, rather than a local time
188:    /// instance, the time zone does not matter and will only be used to
189:    /// display the timestamps, not compare them.
190:    ModifiedDate,
191:
192:    /// The time the file was accessed (the “atime”).
193:    ///
194:    /// Oddly enough, this field rarely holds the *actual* accessed time.
195:    /// Recording a read time means writing to the file each time it’s read
196:    /// slows the whole operation down, so many systems will only update the
197:    /// timestamp in certain circumstances. This has become common enough that
198:    /// it’s now expected behaviour!
199:    /// <https://unix.stackexchange.com/a/8842>
200:    AccessedDate,
201:
202:    /// The time the file was changed (the “ctime”).
203:    ///
204:    /// This field is used to mark the time when a file’s metadata
205:    /// changed — its permissions, owners, or link count.
206:    ///
207:    /// In original Unix, this was, however, meant as creation time.
208:    /// <https://www.bell-labs.com/usr/dmr/www/cacm.html>
209:    ChangedDate,
210:
211:    /// The time the file was created (the “btime” or “birthtime”).
212:    CreatedDate,
213:
214:    /// The type of the file: directories, links, pipes, regular, files, etc.
215:    ///
216:    /// Files are ordered according to the `PartialOrd` implementation of
217:    /// `fs::fields::Type`, so changing that will change this.
218:    FileType,
219:
220:    /// The “age” of the file, which is the time it was modified sorted
221:    /// backwards. The reverse of the `ModifiedDate` ordering!
222:    ///
223:    /// It turns out that listing the most-recently-modified files first is a
224:    /// common-enough use case that it deserves its own variant. This would be
225:    /// implemented by just using the modified date and setting the reverse
226:    /// flag, but this would make reversing *that* output not work, which is
227:    /// bad, even though that’s kind of nonsensical. So it’s its own variant
228:    /// that can be reversed like usual.
229:    ModifiedAge,
230:
231:    /// The file's name, however if the name of the file begins with `.`
232:    /// ignore the leading `.` and then sort as Name
233:    NameMixHidden(SortCase),
234:}
